// syncPolicyFor builds the git-sync policy for one request. Extracted from the
// config literal so the empty-source wiring is unit-testable: every field here
// is a plain bool with a defaulted-off zero value, so dropping any one of them
// compiles, passes, and silently turns the policy off — which is exactly how
// the first version of this shipped inert (AllowEmptySource set, the two
// assertions it requires never supplied).
func syncPolicyFor(req syncRequest) gitsync.SyncPolicy {
	// entiredb-kind only, for both halves of the empty-source policy. The
	// distinction is only sound where a data plane can answer for the
	// repository's ref state, and the watermark it feeds exists solely for
	// native mirrors — a GitHub source gains nothing and would have its
	// empty-repo syncs reclassified from no-op to ref-applying success,
	// moving last_success_unix and pruning the failure index on a sync that
	// applied nothing.
	// The namespace kind is excluded with GitHub: it takes no position
	// snapshot, so it can assert nothing about either leg.
	native := req.kind() == gitjobs.SourceKindEntireDB
	return gitsync.SyncPolicy{
		IncludeTags: true,
		Prune:       true,
		// Ask git-sync to separate "the source has no refs" from "the scope
		// selected none of them", and to treat a source and target both
		// VERIFIED empty as the converged state they are rather than an
		// error. That converged sync stamps a write position, which is what
		// lets entire-core's resync sweep clear a flag on a repo that was
		// created and never pushed to (COR-1300); without it the placement is
		// flagged behind forever, since a no-op completion carries no
		// watermark and the flag can never be satisfied.
		AllowEmptySource: native,
		// An owner that deleted its last namespace branch advertises refs but
		// none in scope; without this the replicas keep serving a branch the
		// owner no longer has.
		AllowEmptyScope: req.namespaceScoped(),
		// The assertions git-sync requires before it will call anything
		// converged: it cannot determine emptiness itself on either leg,
		// because ref hiding is invisible to a client and configured
		// independently per leg, so an empty advertisement and a repository
		// full of hidden refs are the same observation. They come from
		// RepoAdmin.GetWritePosition on each side, read before the fetch (see
		// syncRequest).
		//
		// git-sync treats them as necessary and never sufficient: it still
		// requires an empty advertisement on each leg, an unborn HEAD on the
		// source, and no visible in-scope target ref, and each of those checks
		// can only refuse. So a wrong assertion cannot manufacture a
		// convergence out of a populated repository — but a MISSING one
		// (every read failure lands there) does forfeit a real convergence,
		// which is the direction to fail in.
		//
		// Gated on native as well, so a GitHub request carrying stray
		// assertions cannot half-enable a policy whose other half is off.

